  <!-- 01 · SCARCE -->
  <section class="g-dark" id="scarce">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow" data-v9-reveal="">01 · What’s scarce, by function · illustrative preview</div>
      <h2 data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:60ms">Pick a function. See what’s missing.</h2>
      <p class="lead" data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:120ms">
        This part is ours. When a candidate doesn’t clear the bar, the interviewer records which skill held them back.
        The preview below uses sample figures; the first quarterly note carries the real ones.
      </p>
      <ScarcityLab />
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- 02 · FINDINGS -->
  <section class="g-white" id="findings">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow" data-v9-reveal=""><span class="tick">\</span> 02 · The market, sourced</div>
      <h2 data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:60ms">Four numbers behind the market.</h2>
      <p class="lead" data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:120ms">
        From public research, each with its source. They explain why the same hire now takes more work, and where the
        shortages sit.
      </p>
      <div class="in-finds">
        <article class="in-find" data-v9-reveal="">
          <b class="in-big">319</b><h3>
            applications for every hire, up from 121 in early 2021.<sup class="fn"
              ><a aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Source 1" class="fn-r" href="#src-1">1</a></sup
            >
          </h3><p>Reaching a candidate has never been cheaper. Deciding on one now starts from a far bigger pile.</p>
        </article><article class="in-find" data-v9-reveal="">
          <b class="in-big">17.6</b><h3>
            interviews for every technical hire, up from 11.6 in 2021.<sup class="fn"
              ><a aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Source 2" class="fn-r" href="#src-2">2</a></sup
            >
          </h3><p>
            Half as many interviews again, for the same one hire. Most of that time is spent establishing who was never
            right.
          </p>
        </article><article class="in-find" data-v9-reveal="">
          <b class="in-big">2.3 million</b><h3>
            AI job openings expected in India by 2027.<sup class="fn"
              ><a aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Source 3" class="fn-r" href="#src-3">3</a></sup
            >
          </h3><p>
            The talent pool is forecast at about 1.2 million. For every person who can do the work, there will be close
            to two roles asking for them.
          </p>
        </article><article class="in-find" data-v9-reveal="">
          <b class="in-big">10.7 lakh</b><h3>
            people short in India’s cybersecurity workforce, up 35.9% in a year.<sup class="fn"
              ><a aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Source 4" class="fn-r" href="#src-4">4</a></sup
            >
          </h3><p>And 90% of organisations worldwide report skills gaps inside the security teams they already have.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- 03 · WHERE INDIA IS HIRING -->
  <section class="g-paper" id="where">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow" data-v9-reveal=""><span class="tick">\</span> 03 · Where India is hiring</div>
      <h2 data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:60ms">The demand is real. So is the squeeze.</h2>
      <p class="lead" data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:120ms">
        More centres, more roles, more cities, and recruiters who still close under two technical hires a month.
      </p>
      <div class="in-ws">
        <div class="in-w" data-v9-reveal="">
          <b>2,117</b><p>
            global capability centres in India, employing 2.36 million people.<sup class="fn"
              ><a aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Source 5" class="fn-r" href="#src-5">5</a></sup
            >
          </p>
        </div><div class="in-w" data-v9-reveal="">
          <b>1.35 lakh</b><p>
            net new jobs in India’s tech industry in FY26, taking it to 59.5 lakh people.<sup class="fn"
              ><a aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Source 6" class="fn-r" href="#src-6">6</a></sup
            >
          </p>
        </div><div class="in-w" data-v9-reveal="">
          <b>21%</b><p>
            of everyone in India’s GCCs works in BFSI, close to 3 lakh people.<sup class="fn"
              ><a aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Source 7" class="fn-r" href="#src-7">7</a></sup
            >
          </p>
        </div><div class="in-w" data-v9-reveal="">
          <b>3.8</b><p>
            technical hires per recruiter per quarter. Under two a month.<sup class="fn"
              ><a aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Source 2" class="fn-r" href="#src-2">2</a></sup
            >
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- 04 · METHOD -->
  <section class="g-dark" id="method">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow" data-v9-reveal=""><span class="tick">\</span> 04 · How we know</div>
      <h2 data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:60ms">The method, so you can argue with it.</h2>
      <p class="lead" data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:120ms">
        A number without a method is a marketing claim. Here is where these come from, and what they can’t tell you.
      </p>
      <ol class="in-m">
        <li data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:0ms">
          <span class="ql">The source</span><h3>Every interview is scored.</h3><p>
            30 to 45 minutes with a senior expert who has done the job, scored against a bar agreed before the
            interview. Nothing here is self-reported.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:80ms">
          <span class="ql">The unit</span><h3>Scarcity is counted by skill.</h3><p>
            When someone doesn’t clear, we record which skill held them back. The percentages show what’s scarce in a
            pool. They aren’t a verdict on anyone in it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:160ms">
          <span class="ql">The check</span><h3>Outcomes only where we have them.</h3><p>
            Retention findings come only from searches old enough to have a twelve-month answer, which is a smaller
            sample than the interview counts.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li data-v9-reveal="" style="--d:240ms">
          <span class="ql">The limit</span><h3>It isn’t the whole market.</h3><p>
            These are people who reached a Tribera interview, and our clients’ briefs skew them toward the roles they
            hire for. Transcripts are deleted on schedule. Only anonymous scores are kept.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
